As the person who sat there and created the +49 Mjollnir you're
referring to (this was a fifth level character who had it, too), that
was merely an expirement to see how high I could get it.  Nothing more. 
And, it was a bit of a rush to really whollop the Wizard and the greater
demons.  ("Demogorgon?  No problem!")

> I say that I have no remorse because (a) By the time I care enough to
> dupe save files I've invested 10 hours or so in a character, (b) The
> game can crash unexpectedly, at any time, rubbing out a character that
> may have done nothing wrong, and (c) I don't see how its any different
> from any other game that does have a save feature.

Well, I'd definately agree with (b).  Having lost two characters mere
steps from their coaligned altar on the Astral plain (a Samurai and an
Archaeologist), I can say that having a backup in case of a game crash
is only prudent.  I did have a backup for Arkansas Smith, the
Archaelogist and I ascended him.  I play without save and restore
nowadays because I'm trying to make the game more challenging (and more
painful, too, since it's a real pain in the butt to lose a character
once they've finished the quest).  There isn't anything really wrong
with it, though.  My first 3.1 ascention (a barbarian), I did do a save
and restore because I (stupidly) killed the Wizard over water and didn't
have a way to fish the Book of the Dead out.  So, I restored and lobbed
one of my Amulets of Life Saving into the water as payment.  After all,
it is just a game.  If save and restore makes it more enjoyable, go for
it.  If you think it is morally wrong, don't do it.  Simple.

